window.META_MATH = {"subject": "General Topology", "projects": [{"id": "GT-01", "slug": "continuous-images-preserve-connectedness", "title": "Continuous Images Preserve Connectedness", "target": "<p>For a connected topological space\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X</annotation></semantics></math>\nand continuous\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>:</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>→</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f:X\\to Y</annotation></semantics></math>,\nprove\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(X)</annotation></semantics></math>\nis connected in the subspace topology. Deduce the Intermediate Value\nTheorem for continuous maps from real intervals to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>ℝ</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbb R</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>", "imports": ["<p>A separation of a space is a pair of disjoint nonempty open subsets\ncovering it; connected means there is no separation. Use continuity as\nopenness of inverse images and the subspace topology. Import\nconnectedness of real intervals, as a least-upper-bound consequence; no\nstatement about continuity of an inverse is involved.</p>"], "nodes": [{"id": "N1", "title": "Pull back a putative separation", "proof": "1. Suppose f(X)=U∪V is a separation in its subspace topology.\n\n2. Write U=f(X)∩O and V=f(X)∩P for openO,P⊂Y.\n\n3. Their inverse images are open inX by continuity.\n\n4. Both are nonempty because U,V are nonempty subsets of the image.\n\n5. They are disjoint and coverX.\n\n6. This contradicts connectedness.\n\n7. Therefore f(X) is connected; the empty image, ifX is empty, also has no separation.", "role": "<p>Work in the actual image, not all of Y.</p>", "inputs": "<p>Connected X; continuous\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>:</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>→</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f:X\\to Y</annotation></semantics></math>;\nfoundational imports stated in §1</p>", "export": "<p>Continuous images of connected spaces are connected.</p>", "faculties": {"Ego": "<p>Own E1/v1: Continuous images of connected spaces are connected.</p>", "Memory": "<p>Retain Connected X; continuous\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>:</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>→</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f:X\\to Y</annotation></semantics></math>;\nfoundational imports stated in §1; keep their assumptions and versions\nattached.</p>", "Intellect": "<p>Work in the actual image, not all of Y.</p>", "Sense": "<p>O and P need not be disjoint in Y; their intersections with the image\nare.</p>", "Action": "<p>Carry out the numbered deductions and publish E1/v1 together with its\nhypotheses and proof location.</p>"}, "activities": ["<p>Suppose\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>U</mi><mo>∪</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(X)=U\\cup V</annotation></semantics></math>\nis a separation in its subspace topology.</p>", "<p>Write\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>∩</mo><mi>O</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U=f(X)\\cap O</annotation></semantics></math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>∩</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V=f(X)\\cap P</annotation></semantics></math>\nfor open\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>P</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">O,P\\subset Y</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>", "<p>Their inverse images are open in X by continuity.</p>", "<p>Both are nonempty because U,V are nonempty subsets of the image.</p>", "<p>They are disjoint and coverX.</p>", "<p>This contradicts connectedness.</p>", "<p>Therefore\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(X)</annotation></semantics></math>\nis connected; the empty image, if X is empty, also has no\nseparation.</p>"], "exportId": "E1", "gate": "<p>O and P need not be disjoint in Y; their intersections with the image\nare. Publish only after checking every displayed deduction.</p>", "repair": "<p>If the check fails, revise N1: O and P need not be disjoint in Y;\ntheir intersections with the image are. Recheck dependent nodes N2.</p>"}, {"id": "N2", "title": "Connected subsets of R are intervals", "proof": "1. If s<t belong to a connected subsetC⊂R but y∈(s,t) does not, then C∩(−∞,y) and C∩(y,∞) are disjoint nonempty relative open sets coveringC, a contradiction.\n\n2. Thus C contains every point between any two of its points.\n\n3. For a continuous function on a real interval, connectedness of the domain and node1 make its image such a subset; every value between f(a) andf(b) lies in the image.\n\n4. The endpoint values themselves are already attained.", "role": "<p>Use a missing intermediate point to create a separation.</p>", "inputs": "<p>A connected image in R; prior exports E1/v1</p>", "export": "<p>A topological proof of the intermediate value theorem.</p>", "faculties": {"Ego": "<p>Own E2/v1: A topological proof of the intermediate value theorem.</p>", "Memory": "<p>Retain A connected image in R; prior exports E1/v1; keep their\nassumptions and versions attached.</p>", "Intellect": "<p>Use a missing intermediate point to create a separation.</p>", "Sense": "<p>Connectedness of the codomainY is neither needed nor sufficient.</p>", "Action": "<p>Carry out the numbered deductions and publish E2/v1 together with its\nhypotheses and proof location.</p>"}, "activities": ["<p>If\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s&lt;t</annotation></semantics></math>\nbelong to a connected subset\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C\\subset R</annotation></semantics></math>\nbut\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∈</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y\\in (s,t)</annotation></semantics></math>\ndoes not, then\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>∩</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>−</mi><mi>∞</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C\\cap (-\\infty ,y)</annotation></semantics></math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>∩</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>∞</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C\\cap (y,\\infty )</annotation></semantics></math>\nare disjoint nonempty relative open sets coveringC, a contradiction.</p>", "<p>Thus C contains every point between any two of its points.</p>", "<p>For a continuous function on a real interval, connectedness of the\ndomain and node1 make its image such a subset; every value between\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(a)</annotation></semantics></math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(b)</annotation></semantics></math>\nlies in the image.</p>", "<p>The endpoint values themselves are already attained.</p>"], "exportId": "E2", "gate": "<p>Connectedness of the codomainY is neither needed nor sufficient.\nPublish only after checking every displayed deduction.</p>", "repair": "<p>If the check fails, revise N2: Connectedness of the codomainY is\nneither needed nor sufficient. Withhold this terminal result until its\ngate passes.</p>"}], "edges": [{"source": "N1", "target": "N2", "export": "E1/v1", "id": "T1", "activity": "N2: Connected subsets of R are intervals", "gate": "<p>Use the same mathematical objects and hypotheses as the source\nexport. Receiving contract: A connected image in R; prior exports\nE1/v1</p>"}], "proof": "<p>Read these steps in order as one proof; the diagram is optional.\nNumbered activities identify the specific constructions and deductions\nowned by each node.</p>\n<h3 id=\"n1-pull-back-a-putative-separation-1\">§4.N1 — Pull back a\nputative separation</h3>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li><p>Suppose\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>U</mi><mo>∪</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(X)=U\\cup V</annotation></semantics></math>\nis a separation in its subspace topology.</p></li>\n<li><p>Write\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>∩</mo><mi>O</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U=f(X)\\cap O</annotation></semantics></math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>∩</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V=f(X)\\cap P</annotation></semantics></math>\nfor open\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>P</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">O,P\\subset Y</annotation></semantics></math>.</p></li>\n<li><p>Their inverse images are open in X by continuity.</p></li>\n<li><p>Both are nonempty because U,V are nonempty subsets of the\nimage.</p></li>\n<li><p>They are disjoint and coverX.</p></li>\n<li><p>This contradicts connectedness.</p></li>\n<li><p>Therefore\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(X)</annotation></semantics></math>\nis connected; the empty image, if X is empty, also has no\nseparation.</p></li>\n</ol>\n<h3 id=\"n2-connected-subsets-of-r-are-intervals-1\">§4.N2 — Connected\nsubsets of R are intervals</h3>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li><p>If\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s&lt;t</annotation></semantics></math>\nbelong to a connected subset\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C\\subset R</annotation></semantics></math>\nbut\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∈</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y\\in (s,t)</annotation></semantics></math>\ndoes not, then\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>∩</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>−</mi><mi>∞</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C\\cap (-\\infty ,y)</annotation></semantics></math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>∩</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>∞</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C\\cap (y,\\infty )</annotation></semantics></math>\nare disjoint nonempty relative open sets coveringC, a\ncontradiction.</p></li>\n<li><p>Thus C contains every point between any two of its\npoints.</p></li>\n<li><p>For a continuous function on a real interval, connectedness of\nthe domain and node1 make its image such a subset; every value between\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(a)</annotation></semantics></math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(b)</annotation></semantics></math>\nlies in the image.</p></li>\n<li><p>The endpoint values themselves are already attained.</p></li>\n</ol>", "example": "<p>The continuous map\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mi>o</mi><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(t)=(cost,sint)</annotation></semantics></math>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mi>r</mi><mi>o</mi><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">from[0,2\\pi ]</annotation></semantics></math>\nhas image\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>,\nso the circle is connected. For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(t)=t^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>o</mi><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mi>−</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">on[-1,2]</annotation></semantics></math>,\nthe image[0,4] contains every value between the selected endpoint values\n1 and 4, even though the map is not injective.</p>", "boundary": ["<p>A map from the disconnected two-point discrete space to R with values\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−</mi><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-1</annotation></semantics></math>\nand 1 is continuous but misses0. Sense identifies the domain condition\nrather than assuming the codomain’s connectedness proves connectedness\nof every image.</p>"], "repair": [{"label": "Failure and correction", "html": "<p>This is an authored teaching trace, not a recorded agent run.</p>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li><strong>Ego\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mo>→</mo><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\to</annotation></semantics></math>\nMemory:</strong> register the §1 theorem, imports, and requested exports\nbefore beginning the proof.</li>\n<li><strong>Intellect\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mo>→</mo><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\to</annotation></semantics></math>\nAction:</strong> choose the bridges in §3 and carry out the numbered\nactivities in §4; transmit only established E-exports along the §2\nDAG.</li>\n<li><strong>Sense\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mo>→</mo><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\to</annotation></semantics></math>\nresponsible node:</strong> test the specific boundary/failure in §5 and\nthe node’s individual audit. A failing candidate is withheld; downstream\nnodes may not use it.</li>\n<li><strong>Memory + Intellect:</strong> identify whether the error is\nan omitted condition, an invalid inference, or an attempted extension\nbeyond the theorem. Restore the exact contract or explicitly state a\ndifferent one.</li>\n<li><strong>Action:</strong> redo the affected mathematical activity\nusing the repaired condition or inference. Recheck consuming nodes,\nrather than feeding their conclusions back as premises.</li>\n<li><strong>Ego:</strong> publish only the supported export with its\nassumptions and proof location. A hypothetical revised export is v2; the\nwritten proof here is the v1 exposition, not a claim that this repair\nwas executed.</li>\n</ol>"}, {"label": "Hypothesis boundary", "html": "<p>A map from the disconnected two-point discrete space to R with values\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−</mi><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-1</annotation></semantics></math>\nand 1 is continuous but misses0. Sense identifies the domain condition\nrather than assuming the codomain’s connectedness proves connectedness\nof every image.</p>"}, {"label": "Version and recheck", "html": "<p>Treat any changed assumption as a new contract. Recheck the revised\nexport and all of its descendants. A backward diagnostic never\nestablishes an earlier theorem. This is an authored teaching trace, not\nan execution log.</p>"}], "standard": true, "sha256": "9912b07d4d1f2568ce5c50273cc607b3e2f4cb005228564e1c9666e03772b5a8", "idea": "<p>A complete authored walkthrough of continuous images preserve\nconnectedness, with explicit mathematical contracts, local verification\ngates, and reusable exports.</p>", "references": "<ul>\n<li><a\nhref=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/18-901-introduction-to-topology-fall-2004/pages/lecture-notes/\">MIT\nIntroduction to Topology: separation, Urysohn, and Tychonoff\nmaterial</a>.</li>\n</ul>", "pdf": "reports/pdf/continuous-images-preserve-connectedness.pdf", "tex": "reports/latex/continuous-images-preserve-connectedness.tex", "markdown": "markdown/continuous-images-preserve-connectedness.md"}, {"id": "GT-02", "slug": "compact-hausdorff-homeomorphism-theorem", "title": "Compact-to-Hausdorff Continuous Bijection Theorem", "target": "<p>If X is compact, Y is Hausdorff, and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>:</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>→</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f:X\\to Y</annotation></semantics></math>\nis a continuous bijection, then f is a homeomorphism.</p>\n<p>A homeomorphism is a bijection continuous in both directions. The\ntask is therefore to prove continuity of the inverse. The source X need\nnot initially be assumed Hausdorff; the conclusion makes it homeomorphic\nto a Hausdorff space.</p>", "imports": ["<p>compactness means every open cover has a finite subcover. Hausdorff\nmeans distinct points have disjoint open neighborhoods. A closed map\nsends closed subsets to closed subsets. The proof below derives the\nsupporting compactness lemmas directly from these definitions.</p>\n<p>For the final application only, import compactness of the closed\ninterval, the quotient universal property, and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>s</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">e^{2\\pi it}=e^{2\\pi is}</annotation></semantics></math>\nexactly when\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t-s\\in\\mathbb Z</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>"], "nodes": [{"id": "N1", "title": "Fix the Missing Obligation", "proof": "Bijectivity defines a set-map h=f^(−1):Y→X. Its continuity is not assumed. We will show that for every closed C in X, h^(−1)(C) is closed in Y. This closed-set criterion is equivalent to continuity by taking complements.", "faculties": {"Ego": "<p>Own E1/v1: The inverse set-map\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>−</mi><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>:</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>→</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h=f^{-1}:Y\\to X</annotation></semantics></math>\nexists; it remains to prove its continuity.</p>", "Memory": "<p>Bijection and topology definitions</p>", "Intellect": "<p>Target inverse continuity</p>", "Sense": "<p>Check each hypothesis separately</p>", "Action": "<p>Carry out the numbered deductions and publish E1/v1 together with its\nhypotheses and proof location.</p>"}, "role": "<p>Fix the Missing Obligation. Establish the following local result: The\ninverse set-map\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>−</mi><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>:</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>→</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h=f^{-1}:Y\\to X</annotation></semantics></math>\nexists; it remains to prove its continuity.</p>", "inputs": "<p><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X</annotation></semantics></math>\ncompact,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Y</annotation></semantics></math>\nHausdorff, and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>:</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>→</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f:X\\to Y</annotation></semantics></math>\na continuous bijection.</p>", "export": "<p>The inverse set-map\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>−</mi><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>:</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>→</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h=f^{-1}:Y\\to X</annotation></semantics></math>\nexists; it remains to prove its continuity.</p>", "activities": ["<p>Check compactness, Hausdorffness, continuity, and bijectivity\nseparately.</p>", "<p>Define the inverse as a set-map.</p>", "<p>Reduce inverse continuity to closed inverse images.</p>"], "exportId": "E1", "gate": "<p>Check each hypothesis separately Publish only after checking every\ndisplayed deduction.</p>", "repair": "<p>If the check fails, revise N1: Check each hypothesis separately\nRecheck dependent nodes N2, N3, N4, N5.</p>"}, {"id": "N2", "title": "Closed Subsets and Continuous Images Are Compact", "proof": "Let C be closed in compact X. Any cover of C by relatively open sets can be represented by sets open in X. Add the open complement X\\C. These sets cover X, so finitely many suffice. Removing the complement leaves a finite subcover of C. Thus C is compact.\n\nNow let K be compact and g:K→Z continuous. An open cover of g(K) pulls back under g to an open cover of K. Choose finitely many preimages covering K; their original sets cover g(K). Hence continuous images of compact sets are compact.\n\nApply these two lemmas to f restricted to C. The result is that f(C) is compact in Y.", "faculties": {"Ego": "<p>Own E2/v1: For every closed\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C\\subset X</annotation></semantics></math>,\nthe image\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(C)</annotation></semantics></math>\nis compact.</p>", "Memory": "<p>Open covers and preimages</p>", "Intellect": "<p>Transfer finite-subcover property</p>", "Sense": "<p>Use closed source sets</p>", "Action": "<p>Carry out the numbered deductions and publish E2/v1 together with its\nhypotheses and proof location.</p>"}, "role": "<p>Closed Subsets and Continuous Images Are Compact. Establish the\nfollowing local result: For every closed\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C\\subset X</annotation></semantics></math>,\nthe image\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(C)</annotation></semantics></math>\nis compact.</p>", "inputs": "<p>N1/E1 and the open-cover definition of compactness.</p>", "export": "<p>For every closed\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C\\subset X</annotation></semantics></math>,\nthe image\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(C)</annotation></semantics></math>\nis compact.</p>", "activities": ["<p>Add the open complement of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C</annotation></semantics></math>\nto a cover and use compactness of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>", "<p>Pull an open cover of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(C)</annotation></semantics></math>\nback along the restricted continuous map.</p>", "<p>Push the resulting finite subcover forward.</p>"], "exportId": "E2", "gate": "<p>Use closed source sets Publish only after checking every displayed\ndeduction.</p>", "repair": "<p>If the check fails, revise N2: Use closed source sets Recheck\ndependent nodes N4, N5.</p>"}, {"id": "N3", "title": "Compact Subsets of a Hausdorff Space Are Closed", "proof": "Let K be compact in Hausdorff Y and choose y outside K. For every x in K choose disjoint open neighborhoods U_x of x and V_x of y. Finitely many U_x, say $U_{x_1},\\ldots,U_{x_m}$, cover K. The intersection $V=\\bigcap_{j=1}^mV_{x_j}$ is an open neighborhood of y disjoint from their union and thus from K.\n\nEvery point outside K has such a neighborhood, so Y\\K is open and K is closed. If K is empty, closedness is immediate. Finiteness is essential: an arbitrary intersection of open neighborhoods need not be open.", "faculties": {"Ego": "<p>Own E3/v1: Every compact subset of a Hausdorff space is closed.</p>", "Memory": "<p>Disjoint neighborhoods</p>", "Intellect": "<p>Separate outsider from compact set</p>", "Sense": "<p>Use finite intersections after a finite subcover</p>", "Action": "<p>Carry out the numbered deductions and publish E3/v1 together with its\nhypotheses and proof location.</p>"}, "role": "<p>Compact Subsets of a Hausdorff Space Are Closed. Establish the\nfollowing local result: Every compact subset of a Hausdorff space is\nclosed.</p>", "inputs": "<p>N1/E1 supplies the Hausdorff target; take an arbitrary compact subset\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">K\\subset Y</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>", "export": "<p>Every compact subset of a Hausdorff space is closed.</p>", "activities": ["<p>Separate an outsider from each point of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">K</annotation></semantics></math>\nby disjoint open neighborhoods.</p>", "<p>Use compactness to select finitely many neighborhoods covering\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">K</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>", "<p>Intersect the finitely many outsider neighborhoods to prove the\ncomplement open.</p>"], "exportId": "E3", "gate": "<p>Use finite intersections after a finite subcover Publish only after\nchecking every displayed deduction.</p>", "repair": "<p>If the check fails, revise N3: Use finite intersections after a\nfinite subcover Recheck dependent nodes N4, N5.</p>"}, {"id": "N4", "title": "Close the Inverse-Continuity Bridge", "proof": "For every closed C in X, N2 proves f(C) compact and N3 proves it closed in Y. Therefore f is a closed map. Since h is the inverse bijection,\n\n$$h^{-1}(C)=f(C).$$\n\nThis is closed for every closed C, so h is continuous. The original f was continuous, and thus is a homeomorphism. $\\square$", "faculties": {"Ego": "<p>Own E4/v1:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f</annotation></semantics></math>\nis a homeomorphism.</p>", "Memory": "<p>Closed-set continuity criterion</p>", "Intellect": "<p>Recognize f as closed map</p>", "Sense": "<p>Use bijection to identify preimages</p>", "Action": "<p>Carry out the numbered deductions and publish E4/v1 together with its\nhypotheses and proof location.</p>"}, "role": "<p>Close the Inverse-Continuity Bridge. Establish the following local\nresult:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f</annotation></semantics></math>\nis a homeomorphism.</p>", "inputs": "<p>N1/E1, N2/E2, and N3/E3.</p>", "export": "<p><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f</annotation></semantics></math>\nis a homeomorphism.</p>", "activities": ["<p>Apply the compact-subset lemma to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(C)</annotation></semantics></math>\nfor each closed\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>", "<p>Use\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>−</mi><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h^{-1}(C)=f(C)</annotation></semantics></math>\nto show the inverse is continuous.</p>", "<p>Combine both continuous inverse maps.</p>"], "exportId": "E4", "gate": "<p>Use bijection to identify preimages Publish only after checking every\ndisplayed deduction.</p>", "repair": "<p>If the check fails, revise N4: Use bijection to identify preimages\nRecheck dependent nodes N5.</p>"}, {"id": "N5", "title": "Build a Circle from an Interval", "proof": "Let Q be the quotient of [0,1] identifying only 0 with 1, and let q:[0,1]→Q be the quotient map. Define\n\n$$g:Q\\to S^1,\\qquad g([t])=e^{2\\pi it}.$$\n\nIt is well-defined because the endpoints have the same value. It is continuous by the quotient topology, since g composed with q is continuous. It is onto, and it is one-to-one because equal exponentials imply t−s is an integer; on [0,1] this gives either t=s or the identified endpoint pair.\n\nThe interval is compact, q is continuous and onto, so Q is compact by N2's lemma. The circle is Hausdorff as a subspace of R². N4 therefore proves g is a homeomorphism without needing to analyze inverse continuity separately at the glued endpoint.", "faculties": {"Ego": "<p>Own E5/v1:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo><mi>/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>∼</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">[0,1]/(0\\sim1)</annotation></semantics></math>\nis homeomorphic to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S^1</annotation></semantics></math>\nby\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo><mo>↦</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">[t]\\mapsto e^{2\\pi it}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>", "Memory": "<p>Quotient universal property</p>", "Intellect": "<p>Apply theorem to compact quotient</p>", "Sense": "<p>Check endpoint equivalence exactly</p>", "Action": "<p>Carry out the numbered deductions and publish E5/v1 together with its\nhypotheses and proof location.</p>"}, "role": "<p>Build a Circle from an Interval. Establish the following local\nresult:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo><mi>/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>∼</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">[0,1]/(0\\sim1)</annotation></semantics></math>\nis homeomorphic to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S^1</annotation></semantics></math>\nby\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo><mo>↦</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">[t]\\mapsto e^{2\\pi it}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>", "inputs": "<p>N2/E2, N4/E4, compactness of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>,\nthe quotient topology, and the exponential circle parametrization.</p>", "export": "<p><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo><mi>/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>∼</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">[0,1]/(0\\sim1)</annotation></semantics></math>\nis homeomorphic to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S^1</annotation></semantics></math>\nby\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo><mo>↦</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">[t]\\mapsto e^{2\\pi it}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>", "activities": ["<p>Descend the continuous endpoint-identifying map through the\nquotient.</p>", "<p>Check exactly the endpoint pair is identified and the map is\nonto.</p>", "<p>The quotient is compact and the circle Hausdorff, so the already\nproved theorem applies.</p>"], "exportId": "E5", "gate": "<p>Check endpoint equivalence exactly Publish only after checking every\ndisplayed deduction.</p>", "repair": "<p>If the check fails, revise N5: Check endpoint equivalence exactly\nWithhold this terminal result until its gate passes.</p>"}], "edges": [{"source": "N1", "target": "N2", "export": "E1/v1", "id": "T1", "activity": "N2: Closed Subsets and Continuous Images Are Compact", "gate": "<p>Use the same mathematical objects and hypotheses as the source\nexport. Receiving contract: N1/E1 and the open-cover definition of\ncompactness.</p>"}, {"source": "N1", "target": "N3", "export": "E1/v1", "id": "T2", "activity": "N3: Compact Subsets of a Hausdorff Space Are Closed", "gate": "<p>Use the same mathematical objects and hypotheses as the source\nexport. Receiving contract: N1/E1 supplies the Hausdorff target; take an\narbitrary compact subset\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">K\\subset Y</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>"}, {"source": "N1", "target": "N4", "export": "E1/v1", "id": "T3", "activity": "N4: Close the Inverse-Continuity Bridge", "gate": "<p>Use the same mathematical objects and hypotheses as the source\nexport. Receiving contract: N1/E1, N2/E2, and N3/E3.</p>"}, {"source": "N2", "target": "N4", "export": "E2/v1", "id": "T4", "activity": "N4: Close the Inverse-Continuity Bridge", "gate": "<p>Use the same mathematical objects and hypotheses as the source\nexport. Receiving contract: N1/E1, N2/E2, and N3/E3.</p>"}, {"source": "N3", "target": "N4", "export": "E3/v1", "id": "T5", "activity": "N4: Close the Inverse-Continuity Bridge", "gate": "<p>Use the same mathematical objects and hypotheses as the source\nexport. Receiving contract: N1/E1, N2/E2, and N3/E3.</p>"}, {"source": "N2", "target": "N5", "export": "E2/v1", "id": "T6", "activity": "N5: Build a Circle from an Interval", "gate": "<p>Use the same mathematical objects and hypotheses as the source\nexport. Receiving contract: N2/E2, N4/E4, compactness of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>,\nthe quotient topology, and the exponential circle parametrization.</p>"}, {"source": "N4", "target": "N5", "export": "E4/v1", "id": "T7", "activity": "N5: Build a Circle from an Interval", "gate": "<p>Use the same mathematical objects and hypotheses as the source\nexport. Receiving contract: N2/E2, N4/E4, compactness of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>,\nthe quotient topology, and the exponential circle parametrization.</p>"}], "proof": "<h3 id=\"n1-fix-the-missing-obligation-1\">N1 — Fix the Missing\nObligation</h3>\n<p>Bijectivity defines a set-map\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>−</mi><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>:</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>→</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h=f^{(-1)}:Y\\to X</annotation></semantics></math>.\nIts continuity is not assumed. We will show that for every closed C in\nX,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>−</mi><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h^{(-1)}(C)</annotation></semantics></math>\nis closed in Y. This closed-set criterion is equivalent to continuity by\ntaking complements.</p>\n<h3 id=\"n2-closed-subsets-and-continuous-images-are-compact-1\">N2 —\nClosed Subsets and Continuous Images Are Compact</h3>\n<p>Let C be closed in compact X. Any cover of C by relatively open sets\ncan be represented by sets open in X. Add the open complement\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>\\</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X\\setminus C</annotation></semantics></math>.\nThese sets cover X, so finitely many suffice. Removing the complement\nleaves a finite subcover of C. Thus C is compact.</p>\n<p>Now let K be compact and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>:</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>→</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g:K\\to Z</annotation></semantics></math>\ncontinuous. An open cover of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g(K)</annotation></semantics></math>\npulls back under g to an open cover of K. Choose finitely many preimages\ncovering K; their original sets cover\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g(K)</annotation></semantics></math>.\nHence continuous images of compact sets are compact.</p>\n<p>Apply these two lemmas to f restricted to C. The result is that\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(C)</annotation></semantics></math>\nis compact in Y.</p>\n<h3 id=\"n3-compact-subsets-of-a-hausdorff-space-are-closed-1\">N3 —\nCompact Subsets of a Hausdorff Space Are Closed</h3>\n<p>Let K be compact in Hausdorff Y and choose y outside K. For every x\nin K choose disjoint open neighborhoods\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U_x</annotation></semantics></math>\nof x and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V_x</annotation></semantics></math>\nof y. Finitely many\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U_x</annotation></semantics></math>,\nsay\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U_{x_1},\\ldots,U_{x_m}</annotation></semantics></math>,\ncover K. The intersection\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mo>⋂</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>m</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>V</mi><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V=\\bigcap_{j=1}^mV_{x_j}</annotation></semantics></math>\nis an open neighborhood of y disjoint from their union and thus from\nK.</p>\n<p>Every point outside K has such a neighborhood, so\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>\\</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Y\\setminus K</annotation></semantics></math>\nis open and K is closed. If K is empty, closedness is immediate.\nFiniteness is essential: an arbitrary intersection of open neighborhoods\nneed not be open.</p>\n<h3 id=\"n4-close-the-inverse-continuity-bridge-1\">N4 — Close the\nInverse-Continuity Bridge</h3>\n<p>For every closed C in X, N2 proves\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(C)</annotation></semantics></math>\ncompact and N3 proves it closed in Y. Therefore f is a closed map. Since\nh is the inverse bijection,</p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>−</mi><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mi>.</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h^{-1}(C)=f(C).</annotation></semantics></math></p>\n<p>This is closed for every closed C, so h is continuous. The original f\nwas continuous, and thus is a homeomorphism.\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>▫</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\square</annotation></semantics></math></p>\n<h3 id=\"n5-build-a-circle-from-an-interval-1\">N5 — Build a Circle from\nan Interval</h3>\n<p>Let Q be the quotient of [0,1] identifying only 0 with 1, and let\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>:</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo><mo>→</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q:[0,1]\\to Q</annotation></semantics></math>\nbe the quotient map. Define</p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>:</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo>→</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"></mspace><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></msup><mi>.</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g:Q\\to S^1,\\qquad g([t])=e^{2\\pi it}.</annotation></semantics></math></p>\n<p>It is well-defined because the endpoints have the same value. It is\ncontinuous by the quotient topology, since g composed with q is\ncontinuous. It is onto, and it is one-to-one because equal exponentials\nimply\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t-s</annotation></semantics></math>\nis an integer; on [0,1] this gives either\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t=s</annotation></semantics></math>\nor the identified endpoint pair.</p>\n<p>The interval is compact, q is continuous and onto, so Q is compact by\nN2’s lemma. The circle is Hausdorff as a subspace of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>.\nN4 therefore proves g is a homeomorphism without needing to analyze\ninverse continuity separately at the glued endpoint.</p>", "example": "<p>Let Q be the quotient of [0,1] identifying only 0 with 1, and let\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>:</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo><mo>→</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q:[0,1]\\to Q</annotation></semantics></math>\nbe the quotient map. Define</p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>:</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo>→</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"></mspace><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></msup><mi>.</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g:Q\\to S^1,\\qquad g([t])=e^{2\\pi it}.</annotation></semantics></math></p>\n<p>It is well-defined because the endpoints have the same value. It is\ncontinuous by the quotient topology, since g composed with q is\ncontinuous. It is onto, and it is one-to-one because equal exponentials\nimply\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t-s</annotation></semantics></math>\nis an integer; on [0,1] this gives either\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t=s</annotation></semantics></math>\nor the identified endpoint pair.</p>\n<p>The interval is compact, q is continuous and onto, so Q is compact by\nN2’s lemma. The circle is Hausdorff as a subspace of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>.\nN4 therefore proves g is a homeomorphism without needing to analyze\ninverse continuity separately at the glued endpoint.</p>\n<p>The application consumes the already proved N4 theorem. It supplies\nno premise back to N4.</p>", "boundary": ["<p>Hausdorffness cannot be dropped: the identity from [0,1] with its\nusual topology to the same set with the indiscrete topology is a\ncontinuous bijection from a compact source, but its inverse is not\ncontinuous. Bijectivity also matters: a continuous surjection may\nidentify distinct points and need not have an inverse at all.</p>\n<p>If both spaces are empty the conclusion is immediate. Finite\nintersections, rather than arbitrary intersections, are the decisive use\nof compactness in N3.</p>\n<p>Export the closed-subset compactness lemma, continuous-image\ncompactness lemma, Hausdorff compact-set closedness lemma, and the\ncontinuous-bijection criterion. The circle application supplies a\nconcrete quotient-space example for later algebraic topology\nconstructions.</p>"], "repair": [{"label": "Failure and correction", "html": "<p>Consider the continuous bijection\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>:</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>→</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f:[0,2\\pi)\\to S^1</annotation></semantics></math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(t)=e^{it}</annotation></semantics></math>.\nThe source is not compact. Points\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mi>/</mi><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">e^{i(2\\pi-1/n)}</annotation></semantics></math>\napproach 1, but their inverse values approach\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2\\pi</annotation></semantics></math>\nrather than\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>−</mi><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f^{(-1)}(1)=0</annotation></semantics></math>.\nThus the inverse is discontinuous.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><p><strong>Node:</strong> N1</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>State:</strong> STALLED</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Failing faculty:</strong> Sense</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Failed hypothesis:</strong> compact source</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Observed:</strong> half-open angle interval</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Repair target:</strong> source topology/model</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Blocked export:</strong> homeomorphism</p></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Memory recalls the compactness requirement. Sense isolates the cut at\nangle zero. Intellect replaces the half-open representative model by the\ncompact endpoint quotient. Action verifies the quotient map’s continuity\nand bijectivity. Ego publishes the corrected homeomorphism and preserves\nthe original counterexample as an explanation of why the repair was\nneeded.</p>"}, {"label": "Hypothesis boundary", "html": "<p>Hausdorffness cannot be dropped: the identity from [0,1] with its\nusual topology to the same set with the indiscrete topology is a\ncontinuous bijection from a compact source, but its inverse is not\ncontinuous. Bijectivity also matters: a continuous surjection may\nidentify distinct points and need not have an inverse at all.</p>\n<p>If both spaces are empty the conclusion is immediate. Finite\nintersections, rather than arbitrary intersections, are the decisive use\nof compactness in N3.</p>\n<p>Export the closed-subset compactness lemma, continuous-image\ncompactness lemma, Hausdorff compact-set closedness lemma, and the\ncontinuous-bijection criterion. The circle application supplies a\nconcrete quotient-space example for later algebraic topology\nconstructions.</p>"}, {"label": "Version and recheck", "html": "<p>Treat any changed assumption as a new contract. Recheck the revised\nexport and all of its descendants. A backward diagnostic never\nestablishes an earlier theorem. This is an authored teaching trace, not\nan execution log.</p>"}], "standard": false, "sha256": "75182cf699665812339e2d2802af91cec92e4f4201da7de000facc203bc1fdc8", "idea": "<p>A complete authored walkthrough of compact-to-hausdorff continuous\nbijection theorem, with explicit mathematical contracts, local\nverification gates, and reusable exports.</p>", "references": "<p><a\nhref=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/18-901-introduction-to-topology-fall-2004/pages/lecture-notes/\">MIT\nIntroduction to Topology: compactness and separation material</a>.</p>", "pdf": "reports/pdf/compact-hausdorff-homeomorphism-theorem.pdf", "tex": "reports/latex/compact-hausdorff-homeomorphism-theorem.tex", "markdown": "markdown/compact-hausdorff-homeomorphism-theorem.md"}, {"id": "GT-03", "slug": "urysohns-lemma", "title": "Urysohn’s Lemma", "target": "<p>For a normal\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T_1</annotation></semantics></math>\nspace\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X</annotation></semantics></math>\nand disjoint closed sets\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A,B</annotation></semantics></math>,\nprove a continuous\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>:</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>→</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f:X\\to[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>\nexists with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f=0</annotation></semantics></math>\non\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A</annotation></semantics></math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f=1</annotation></semantics></math>\non\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>", "imports": ["<p>Normal means every pair of disjoint closed sets has disjoint open\nneighborhoods;\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>\nmeans points are closed. Under this convention derive the shrinking\nlemma below. Dyadic rationals are dense in[0,1]. The construction uses\nclosures in X and no metric. EmptyA or B is allowed.</p>"], "nodes": [{"id": "N1", "title": "Normal-space shrinking lemma", "proof": "1. Normality gives disjoint openU,V containingF andX\\G.\n\n2. Since U⊂X\\V andX\\V is closed, closure(U)⊂X\\V⊂G.\n\n3. Thus F⊂U⊂closure(U)⊂G.\n\n4. Take G=X\\B andF=A to chooseU₀ withA⊂U₀⊂closure(U₀)⊂U₁=X\\B.", "role": "<p>Separate F from the closed complement.</p>", "inputs": "<p>ClosedF inside open G; foundational imports stated in §1</p>", "export": "<p>A neighborhood whose closure stays inside the prescribed open\nset.</p>", "faculties": {"Ego": "<p>Own E1/v1: A neighborhood whose closure stays inside the prescribed\nopen set.</p>", "Memory": "<p>Retain ClosedF inside open G; foundational imports stated in §1; keep\ntheir assumptions and versions attached.</p>", "Intellect": "<p>Separate F from the closed complement.</p>", "Sense": "<p>Disjoint open neighborhoods alone are insufficient unless the closure\ninclusion is obtained.</p>", "Action": "<p>Carry out the numbered deductions and publish E1/v1 together with its\nhypotheses and proof location.</p>"}, "activities": ["<p>Normality gives disjoint open U,V containing F and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>\\</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X\\setminus G</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>", "<p>Since\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>\\</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U\\subset X\\setminus V</annotation></semantics></math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>\\</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X\\setminus V</annotation></semantics></math>\nis closed,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">closure</mi><mo>&#8289;</mo></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>⊂</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>\\</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\operatorname{closure} (U)\\subset X\\setminus V\\subset G</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>", "<p>Thus\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi>U</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">closure</mi><mo>&#8289;</mo></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>⊂</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F\\subset U\\subset \\operatorname{closure} (U)\\subset G</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>", "<p>Take\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>\\</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">G=X\\setminus B</annotation></semantics></math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F=A</annotation></semantics></math>\nto choose\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>\nwith\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">closure</mi><mo>&#8289;</mo></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>\\</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A\\subset U_{0}\\subset \\operatorname{closure} (U_{0})\\subset U_{1}=X\\setminus B</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>"], "exportId": "E1", "gate": "<p>Disjoint open neighborhoods alone are insufficient unless the closure\ninclusion is obtained. Publish only after checking every displayed\ndeduction.</p>", "repair": "<p>If the check fails, revise N1: Disjoint open neighborhoods alone are\ninsufficient unless the closure inclusion is obtained. Recheck dependent\nnodes N2, N3, N4.</p>"}, {"id": "N2", "title": "Nested dyadic neighborhoods", "proof": "1. At each finite dyadic stage, for every consecutive pairr<s already constructed chooseU_(r+s)/2 usingF=closure(U_r)⊂G=U_s.\n\n2. Thus closure(U_r)⊂U_mid⊂closure(U_mid)⊂U_s.\n\n3. Induction definesU_r for all dyadicr∈[0,1], and for anyr<s their levels share a sufficiently fine stage, givingclosure(U_r)⊂U_s.\n\n4. AllU_r withr<1 lie inX\\B.", "role": "<p>Insert midpoint neighborhoods between neighboring dyadics.</p>", "inputs": "<p>InitialU\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi></mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">_{0},U_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>\nand shrinking lemma; prior exports E1/v1</p>", "export": "<p>A family with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">closure</mi><mo>&#8289;</mo></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\operatorname{closure} (U_r)\\subset U_s</annotation></semantics></math>\nwhenever\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r&lt;s</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>", "faculties": {"Ego": "<p>Own E2/v1: A family with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">closure</mi><mo>&#8289;</mo></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\operatorname{closure} (U_r)\\subset U_s</annotation></semantics></math>\nwhenever\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r&lt;s</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>", "Memory": "<p>Retain InitialU\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi></mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">_{0},U_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>\nand shrinking lemma; prior exports E1/v1; keep their assumptions and\nversions attached.</p>", "Intellect": "<p>Insert midpoint neighborhoods between neighboring dyadics.</p>", "Sense": "<p>Bare\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>c</mi><mi>l</mi><mi>u</mi><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>o</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>s</mi><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">inclusionsU_r\\subset U_s</annotation></semantics></math>\ndo not supply the continuity argument.</p>", "Action": "<p>Carry out the numbered deductions and publish E2/v1 together with its\nhypotheses and proof location.</p>"}, "activities": ["<p>At each finite dyadic stage, for every consecutive\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>r</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">pairr&lt;s</annotation></semantics></math>\nalready constructed choose\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msub><mi>/</mi><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U_{r+s}/2</annotation></semantics></math>\nusing\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">closure</mi><mo>&#8289;</mo></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>⊂</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F=\\operatorname{closure} (U_r)\\subset G=U_s</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>", "<p>Thus\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">closure</mi><mo>&#8289;</mo></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mi>i</mi><mi>d</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">closure</mi><mo>&#8289;</mo></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mi>i</mi><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\operatorname{closure} (U_r)\\subset U_mid\\subset \\operatorname{closure} (U_mid)\\subset U_s</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>", "<p>Induction defines\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U_r</annotation></semantics></math>\nfor all dyadicr\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>∈</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\in [0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>,\nand for\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>y</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">anyr&lt;s</annotation></semantics></math>\ntheir levels share a sufficiently fine stage, giving\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">closure</mi><mo>&#8289;</mo></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\operatorname{closure} (U_r)\\subset U_s</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>", "<p>All\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U_r</annotation></semantics></math>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>h</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">withr&lt;1</annotation></semantics></math>\nlie in\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>\\</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X\\setminus B</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>"], "exportId": "E2", "gate": "<p>Bare\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>c</mi><mi>l</mi><mi>u</mi><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>o</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>s</mi><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">inclusionsU_r\\subset U_s</annotation></semantics></math>\ndo not supply the continuity argument. Publish only after checking every\ndisplayed deduction.</p>", "repair": "<p>If the check fails, revise N2: Bare\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>c</mi><mi>l</mi><mi>u</mi><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>o</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>s</mi><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">inclusionsU_r\\subset U_s</annotation></semantics></math>\ndo not supply the continuity argument. Recheck dependent nodes N3,\nN4.</p>"}, {"id": "N3", "title": "Function and endpoint values", "proof": "1. Let f(x)=inf{r∈D:x∈U_r}, taking the value1 if the set is empty, whereD is the set of dyadics in[0,1].\n\n2. Then0≤f≤1.\n\n3. Forx∈A, x∈U₀, hencef=0.\n\n4. Forx∈B noU_r containsx, evenU₁=X\\B, so f=1.", "role": "<p>Encode membership by an infimum.</p>", "inputs": "<p>Nested neighborhoods; prior exports E2/v1</p>", "export": "<p>A function into[0,1] taking the specified endpoint values.</p>", "faculties": {"Ego": "<p>Own E3/v1: A function into[0,1] taking the specified endpoint\nvalues.</p>", "Memory": "<p>Retain Nested neighborhoods; prior exports E2/v1; keep their\nassumptions and versions attached.</p>", "Intellect": "<p>Encode membership by an infimum.</p>", "Sense": "<p>The empty-infimum convention must be specified.</p>", "Action": "<p>Carry out the numbered deductions and publish E3/v1 together with its\nhypotheses and proof location.</p>"}, "activities": ["<p>Let\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">inf</mi><mo>&#8289;</mo></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">{</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>D</mi><mo>:</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(x)=\\operatorname{inf} \\{r\\in D:x\\in U_r\\}</annotation></semantics></math>,\ntaking the value 1 if the set is empty, where D is the set of dyadics\nin[0,1].</p>", "<p>Then\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0\\le f\\le 1</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>", "<p>For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x\\in A</annotation></semantics></math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x\\in U_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>,\nhence\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f=0</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>", "<p>For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x\\in B</annotation></semantics></math>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mi>o</mi><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">noU_r</annotation></semantics></math>\ncontainsx, evenU\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi></mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>\\</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">_{1}=X\\setminus B</annotation></semantics></math>,\nso\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f=1</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>"], "exportId": "E3", "gate": "<p>The empty-infimum convention must be specified. Publish only after\nchecking every displayed deduction.</p>", "repair": "<p>If the check fails, revise N3: The empty-infimum convention must be\nspecified. Recheck dependent nodes N4.</p>"}, {"id": "N4", "title": "Continuity via strict level sets", "proof": "1. For0<a≤1, {f<a}=∪_{r∈D,r<a}U_r: membership on either side follows from the infimum and the existence of a dyadic membership level belowa.\n\n2. For0≤a<1, {f>a}=∪_{r∈D,a<r<1}(X\\closure(U_r)).\n\n3. Ifx is outsideclosure(U_r), it belongs to noU_s withs≤r, so f(x)≥r>a.\n\n4. Conversely if f(x)>a choose dyadicsa<r<s<f(x), possible even whenf=1.\n\n5. Thenx∉U_s andclosure(U_r)⊂U_s, sox lies in that open complement.\n\n6. Level sets outside[0,1] are trivial.\n\n7. Both strict level types are open, proving continuity and Urysohn’s lemma.", "role": "<p>Prove both types of strict level sets are open.</p>", "inputs": "<p>Infimum definition and closure nesting; prior exports E2/v1,\nE3/v1</p>", "export": "<p>The required continuous separator.</p>", "faculties": {"Ego": "<p>Own E4/v1: The required continuous separator.</p>", "Memory": "<p>Retain Infimum definition and closure nesting; prior exports E2/v1,\nE3/v1; keep their assumptions and versions attached.</p>", "Intellect": "<p>Prove both types of strict level sets are open.</p>", "Sense": "<p>Superlevel openness uses closures, not just open neighborhoods.</p>", "Action": "<p>Carry out the numbered deductions and publish E4/v1 together with its\nhypotheses and proof location.</p>"}, "activities": ["<p>For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0&lt;a\\le 1</annotation></semantics></math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">{</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">}</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>D</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\{f&lt;a\\}=\\cup _{r\\in D,r&lt;a}U_r</annotation></semantics></math>:\nmembership on either side follows from the infimum and the existence of\na dyadic membership level belowa.</p>", "<p>For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0\\le a&lt;1</annotation></semantics></math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">{</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">}</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>D</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>\\</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">cl</mi><mo>&#8289;</mo></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\{f&gt;a\\}=\\cup _{r\\in D,a&lt;r&lt;1}(X\\setminus\\operatorname{cl}(U_r))</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>", "<p>Ifx is outside\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">closure</mi><mo>&#8289;</mo></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\operatorname{closure} (U_r)</annotation></semantics></math>,\nit belongs to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mi>o</mi><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">noU_s</annotation></semantics></math>\nwith\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s\\le r</annotation></semantics></math>,\nso\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>≥</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(x)\\ge r&gt;a</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>", "<p>Conversely if\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(x)&gt;a</annotation></semantics></math>\nchoose\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>y</mi><mi>a</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>c</mi><mi>s</mi><mi>a</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">dyadicsa&lt;r&lt;s&lt;f(x)</annotation></semantics></math>,\npossible even when\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f=1</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>", "<p>Then\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∉</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x\\notin U_s</annotation></semantics></math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">closure</mi><mo>&#8289;</mo></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\operatorname{closure} (U_r)\\subset U_s</annotation></semantics></math>,\nsox lies in that open complement.</p>", "<p>Level sets outside[0,1] are trivial.</p>", "<p>Both strict level types are open, proving continuity and Urysohn’s\nlemma.</p>"], "exportId": "E4", "gate": "<p>Superlevel openness uses closures, not just open neighborhoods.\nPublish only after checking every displayed deduction.</p>", "repair": "<p>If the check fails, revise N4: Superlevel openness uses closures, not\njust open neighborhoods. Withhold this terminal result until its gate\npasses.</p>"}], "edges": [{"source": "N1", "target": "N2", "export": "E1/v1", "id": "T1", "activity": "N2: Nested dyadic neighborhoods", "gate": "<p>Use the same mathematical objects and hypotheses as the source\nexport. Receiving contract: InitialU\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi></mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">_{0},U_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>\nand shrinking lemma; prior exports E1/v1</p>"}, {"source": "N2", "target": "N3", "export": "E2/v1", "id": "T2", "activity": "N3: Function and endpoint values", "gate": "<p>Use the same mathematical objects and hypotheses as the source\nexport. Receiving contract: Nested neighborhoods; prior exports\nE2/v1</p>"}, {"source": "N2", "target": "N4", "export": "E2/v1", "id": "T3", "activity": "N4: Continuity via strict level sets", "gate": "<p>Use the same mathematical objects and hypotheses as the source\nexport. Receiving contract: Infimum definition and closure nesting;\nprior exports E2/v1, E3/v1</p>"}, {"source": "N3", "target": "N4", "export": "E3/v1", "id": "T4", "activity": "N4: Continuity via strict level sets", "gate": "<p>Use the same mathematical objects and hypotheses as the source\nexport. Receiving contract: Infimum definition and closure nesting;\nprior exports E2/v1, E3/v1</p>"}], "proof": "<p>Read these steps in order as one proof; the diagram is optional.\nNumbered activities identify the specific constructions and deductions\nowned by each node.</p>\n<h3 id=\"n1-normal-space-shrinking-lemma-1\">§4.N1 — Normal-space\nshrinking lemma</h3>\n<p>First let\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F</annotation></semantics></math>\nbe closed and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">G</annotation></semantics></math>\nopen with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F\\subset G</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li><p>Normality gives disjoint open U,V containing F and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>\\</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X\\setminus G</annotation></semantics></math>.</p></li>\n<li><p>Since\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>\\</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U\\subset X\\setminus V</annotation></semantics></math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>\\</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X\\setminus V</annotation></semantics></math>\nis closed,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">closure</mi><mo>&#8289;</mo></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>⊂</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>\\</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\operatorname{closure} (U)\\subset X\\setminus V\\subset G</annotation></semantics></math>.</p></li>\n<li><p>Thus\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi>U</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">closure</mi><mo>&#8289;</mo></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>⊂</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F\\subset U\\subset \\operatorname{closure} (U)\\subset G</annotation></semantics></math>.</p></li>\n<li><p>Take\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>\\</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">G=X\\setminus B</annotation></semantics></math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F=A</annotation></semantics></math>\nto choose\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>\nwith\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">closure</mi><mo>&#8289;</mo></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>\\</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A\\subset U_{0}\\subset \\operatorname{closure} (U_{0})\\subset U_{1}=X\\setminus B</annotation></semantics></math>.</p></li>\n</ol>\n<h3 id=\"n2-nested-dyadic-neighborhoods-1\">§4.N2 — Nested dyadic\nneighborhoods</h3>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li><p>At each finite dyadic stage, for every consecutive\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>r</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">pairr&lt;s</annotation></semantics></math>\nalready constructed choose\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msub><mi>/</mi><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U_{r+s}/2</annotation></semantics></math>\nusing\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">closure</mi><mo>&#8289;</mo></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>⊂</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F=\\operatorname{closure} (U_r)\\subset G=U_s</annotation></semantics></math>.</p></li>\n<li><p>Thus\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">closure</mi><mo>&#8289;</mo></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mi>i</mi><mi>d</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">closure</mi><mo>&#8289;</mo></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mi>i</mi><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\operatorname{closure} (U_r)\\subset U_mid\\subset \\operatorname{closure} (U_mid)\\subset U_s</annotation></semantics></math>.</p></li>\n<li><p>Induction defines\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U_r</annotation></semantics></math>\nfor all dyadicr\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>∈</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\in [0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>,\nand for\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>y</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">anyr&lt;s</annotation></semantics></math>\ntheir levels share a sufficiently fine stage, giving\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">closure</mi><mo>&#8289;</mo></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\operatorname{closure} (U_r)\\subset U_s</annotation></semantics></math>.</p></li>\n<li><p>All\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U_r</annotation></semantics></math>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>h</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">withr&lt;1</annotation></semantics></math>\nlie in\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>\\</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X\\setminus B</annotation></semantics></math>.</p></li>\n</ol>\n<h3 id=\"n3-function-and-endpoint-values-1\">§4.N3 — Function and endpoint\nvalues</h3>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li><p>Let\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">inf</mi><mo>&#8289;</mo></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">{</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>D</mi><mo>:</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(x)=\\operatorname{inf} \\{r\\in D:x\\in U_r\\}</annotation></semantics></math>,\ntaking the value 1 if the set is empty, where D is the set of dyadics\nin[0,1].</p></li>\n<li><p>Then\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0\\le f\\le 1</annotation></semantics></math>.</p></li>\n<li><p>For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x\\in A</annotation></semantics></math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x\\in U_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>,\nhence\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f=0</annotation></semantics></math>.</p></li>\n<li><p>For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x\\in B</annotation></semantics></math>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mi>o</mi><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">noU_r</annotation></semantics></math>\ncontainsx, evenU\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi></mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>\\</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">_{1}=X\\setminus B</annotation></semantics></math>,\nso\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f=1</annotation></semantics></math>.</p></li>\n</ol>\n<h3 id=\"n4-continuity-via-strict-level-sets-1\">§4.N4 — Continuity via\nstrict level sets</h3>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li><p>For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0&lt;a\\le 1</annotation></semantics></math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">{</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">}</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>D</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\{f&lt;a\\}=\\cup _{r\\in D,r&lt;a}U_r</annotation></semantics></math>:\nmembership on either side follows from the infimum and the existence of\na dyadic membership level belowa.</p></li>\n<li><p>For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0\\le a&lt;1</annotation></semantics></math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">{</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">}</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>D</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>\\</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">cl</mi><mo>&#8289;</mo></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\{f&gt;a\\}=\\cup _{r\\in D,a&lt;r&lt;1}(X\\setminus\\operatorname{cl}(U_r))</annotation></semantics></math>.</p></li>\n<li><p>Ifx is outside\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">closure</mi><mo>&#8289;</mo></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\operatorname{closure} (U_r)</annotation></semantics></math>,\nit belongs to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mi>o</mi><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">noU_s</annotation></semantics></math>\nwith\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s\\le r</annotation></semantics></math>,\nso\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>≥</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(x)\\ge r&gt;a</annotation></semantics></math>.</p></li>\n<li><p>Conversely if\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(x)&gt;a</annotation></semantics></math>\nchoose\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>y</mi><mi>a</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>c</mi><mi>s</mi><mi>a</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">dyadicsa&lt;r&lt;s&lt;f(x)</annotation></semantics></math>,\npossible even when\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f=1</annotation></semantics></math>.</p></li>\n<li><p>Then\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∉</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x\\notin U_s</annotation></semantics></math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">closure</mi><mo>&#8289;</mo></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\operatorname{closure} (U_r)\\subset U_s</annotation></semantics></math>,\nsox lies in that open complement.</p></li>\n<li><p>Level sets outside[0,1] are trivial.</p></li>\n<li><p>Both strict level types are open, proving continuity and\nUrysohn’s lemma.</p></li>\n</ol>", "example": "<p>In R take\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">}</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A=\\{0\\},B=\\{1\\}</annotation></semantics></math>;\na concrete separator is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">max</mi><mo>&#8289;</mo></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">min</mi><mo>&#8289;</mo></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(x)=\\operatorname{max} (0,\\operatorname{min} (1,x))</annotation></semantics></math>.\nThe dyadic construction is needed for arbitrary normal spaces; it need\nnot reproduce this particular function. If A is empty the constant 1\nworks, and if B is empty the constant 0 works.</p>", "boundary": ["<p>A candidate drops closure nesting and asserts an arbitrary\nmembership-threshold function is continuous. Sense identifies that its\nsuperlevel sets may fail to be open. Repair the neighborhood\nconstruction before publishing the function; all later continuity claims\ndepend on it.</p>"], "repair": [{"label": "Failure and correction", "html": "<p>This is an authored teaching trace, not a recorded agent run.</p>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li><strong>Ego\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mo>→</mo><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\to</annotation></semantics></math>\nMemory:</strong> register the §1 theorem, imports, and requested exports\nbefore beginning the proof.</li>\n<li><strong>Intellect\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mo>→</mo><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\to</annotation></semantics></math>\nAction:</strong> choose the bridges in §3 and carry out the numbered\nactivities in §4; transmit only established E-exports along the §2\nDAG.</li>\n<li><strong>Sense\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mo>→</mo><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\to</annotation></semantics></math>\nresponsible node:</strong> test the specific boundary/failure in §5 and\nthe node’s individual audit. A failing candidate is withheld; downstream\nnodes may not use it.</li>\n<li><strong>Memory + Intellect:</strong> identify whether the error is\nan omitted condition, an invalid inference, or an attempted extension\nbeyond the theorem. Restore the exact contract or explicitly state a\ndifferent one.</li>\n<li><strong>Action:</strong> redo the affected mathematical activity\nusing the repaired condition or inference. Recheck consuming nodes,\nrather than feeding their conclusions back as premises.</li>\n<li><strong>Ego:</strong> publish only the supported export with its\nassumptions and proof location. A hypothetical revised export is v2; the\nwritten proof here is the v1 exposition, not a claim that this repair\nwas executed.</li>\n</ol>"}, {"label": "Hypothesis boundary", "html": "<p>A candidate drops closure nesting and asserts an arbitrary\nmembership-threshold function is continuous. Sense identifies that its\nsuperlevel sets may fail to be open. Repair the neighborhood\nconstruction before publishing the function; all later continuity claims\ndepend on it.</p>"}, {"label": "Version and recheck", "html": "<p>Treat any changed assumption as a new contract. Recheck the revised\nexport and all of its descendants. A backward diagnostic never\nestablishes an earlier theorem. This is an authored teaching trace, not\nan execution log.</p>"}], "standard": true, "sha256": "f9e499844a5b832c3b661b029ff355f6b3715d716889fbf2abeaa4d9b58e7c87", "idea": "<p>A complete authored walkthrough of urysohn’s lemma, with explicit\nmathematical contracts, local verification gates, and reusable\nexports.</p>", "references": "<ul>\n<li><a\nhref=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/18-901-introduction-to-topology-fall-2004/pages/lecture-notes/\">MIT\nIntroduction to Topology: separation, Urysohn, and Tychonoff\nmaterial</a>.</li>\n</ul>", "pdf": "reports/pdf/urysohns-lemma.pdf", "tex": "reports/latex/urysohns-lemma.tex", "markdown": "markdown/urysohns-lemma.md"}, {"id": "GT-04", "slug": "baire-category-theorem-for-complete-metric-spaces", "title": "Baire Category Theorem for Complete Metric Spaces", "target": "<p>For a nonempty complete metric space\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X</annotation></semantics></math>,\nprove every countable intersection of open dense subsets is dense.\nEquivalently, no nonempty open subset is a countable union of nowhere\ndense subsets.</p>", "imports": ["<p>X is a nonempty complete metric space. Dense means every nonempty\nopen set meets the set. Nowhere dense means the closure has empty\ninterior. Use the fact that every open neighborhood of a point contains\na closed ball of sufficiently small positive radius. Compactness of such\nballs is not assumed.</p>"], "nodes": [{"id": "N1", "title": "Nested closed balls", "proof": "1. Choose a positive-radius closed ballB₁ contained inU∩G₁, withr₁≤1/2.\n\n2. This intersection is nonempty open.\n\n3. Inductively the open ball centered atx_n with radiusr_n meetsG_(n+1); choose a point there and a positive radiusr_(n+1)≤2^(−n−1) so its closed ballB_(n+1) is contained in that intersection.\n\n4. Then B_(n+1)⊂B_n andB_n⊂U∩G₁∩⋯∩G_n.\n\n5. This works also at isolated points, where small balls may be singletons.", "role": "<p>Place each next ball inside the previous interior and the next dense\nopen set.</p>", "inputs": "<p>Open dense\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>…</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">G_{1},G_{2},\\ldots</annotation></semantics></math>;\narbitrary nonempty open U; foundational imports stated in §1</p>", "export": "<p>Nested nonempty closed balls with radii tending to 0 and all required\ninclusions.</p>", "faculties": {"Ego": "<p>Own E1/v1: Nested nonempty closed balls with radii tending to 0 and\nall required inclusions.</p>", "Memory": "<p>Retain Open dense\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>…</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">G_{1},G_{2},\\ldots</annotation></semantics></math>;\narbitrary nonempty open U; foundational imports stated in §1; keep their\nassumptions and versions attached.</p>", "Intellect": "<p>Place each next ball inside the previous interior and the next dense\nopen set.</p>", "Sense": "<p>Do not invoke compactness of closed balls in a general complete\nmetric space.</p>", "Action": "<p>Carry out the numbered deductions and publish E1/v1 together with its\nhypotheses and proof location.</p>"}, "activities": ["<p>Choose a positive-radius closed ball\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>\ncontained in\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>∩</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U\\cap G_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>,\nwith\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn><mi>/</mi><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r_{1}\\le 1/2</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>", "<p>This intersection is nonempty open.</p>", "<p>Inductively the open ball centered at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_n</annotation></semantics></math>\nwith radius\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r_n</annotation></semantics></math>\nmeets\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">G_{n+1}</annotation></semantics></math>;\nchoose a point there and a positive radius\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>≤</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>−</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r_{n+1}\\le 2^{(-n-1)}</annotation></semantics></math>\nso its closed ball\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B_{n+1}</annotation></semantics></math>\nis contained in that intersection.</p>", "<p>Then\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B_{n+1}\\subset B_n</annotation></semantics></math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>⊂</mo><mi>U</mi><mo>∩</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∩</mo><mi>⋯</mi><mo>∩</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B_n\\subset U\\cap G_{1}\\cap \\cdots \\cap G_n</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>", "<p>This works also at isolated points, where small balls may be\nsingletons.</p>"], "exportId": "E1", "gate": "<p>Do not invoke compactness of closed balls in a general complete\nmetric space. Publish only after checking every displayed deduction.</p>", "repair": "<p>If the check fails, revise N1: Do not invoke compactness of closed\nballs in a general complete metric space. Recheck dependent nodes N2,\nN3.</p>"}, {"id": "N2", "title": "Completeness supplies a common point", "proof": "1. For m>n, x_m lies inB_n, so d(x_m,x_n)≤r_n→0.\n\n2. Thus the centers are Cauchy and converge tox∈X by completeness.\n\n3. For fixedn, all subsequent centers lie in the closed setB_n, sox∈B_n.\n\n4. Consequentlyx lies inU and everyG_n.\n\n5. Since U was any nonempty open set, ∩G_n is dense.", "role": "<p>Control centers by nesting and use closedness.</p>", "inputs": "<p>Nested balls and shrinking radii; prior exports E1/v1</p>", "export": "<p>Every countable intersection of open dense sets is dense.</p>", "faculties": {"Ego": "<p>Own E2/v1: Every countable intersection of open dense sets is\ndense.</p>", "Memory": "<p>Retain Nested balls and shrinking radii; prior exports E1/v1; keep\ntheir assumptions and versions attached.</p>", "Intellect": "<p>Control centers by nesting and use closedness.</p>", "Sense": "<p>Vanishing consecutive increments alone would not have supplied this\nCauchy estimate.</p>", "Action": "<p>Carry out the numbered deductions and publish E2/v1 together with its\nhypotheses and proof location.</p>"}, "activities": ["<p>For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m&gt;n</annotation></semantics></math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_m</annotation></semantics></math>\nlies in\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B_n</annotation></semantics></math>,\nso\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d(x_m,x_n)\\le r_n\\to 0</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>", "<p>Thus the centers are Cauchy and converge to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x\\in X</annotation></semantics></math>\nby completeness.</p>", "<p>For fixedn, all subsequent centers lie in the closed set\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B_n</annotation></semantics></math>,\nso\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x\\in B_n</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>", "<p>Consequentlyx lies in U and every\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">G_n</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>", "<p>Since U was any nonempty open set,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>∩</mi><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\cap G_n</annotation></semantics></math>\nis dense.</p>"], "exportId": "E2", "gate": "<p>Vanishing consecutive increments alone would not have supplied this\nCauchy estimate. Publish only after checking every displayed\ndeduction.</p>", "repair": "<p>If the check fails, revise N2: Vanishing consecutive increments alone\nwould not have supplied this Cauchy estimate. Recheck dependent nodes\nN3.</p>"}, {"id": "N3", "title": "Category reformulation", "proof": "1. If A_n are nowhere dense, G_n=X\\closure(A_n) are open dense.\n\n2. Their intersection meets every nonempty openU, soU cannot be covered by∪A_n.\n\n3. Conversely, if some open denseG_n have intersection missing a nonempty openU, thenU⊂∪(X\\G_n), each complement closed with empty interior, contradicting the category property.\n\n4. Thus the formulations are equivalent.", "role": "<p>Take complements of closures.</p>", "inputs": "<p>Dense-intersection theorem and nowhere-dense definition; prior\nexports E2/v1</p>", "export": "<p>The stated Baire theorem and category equivalence.</p>", "faculties": {"Ego": "<p>Own E3/v1: The stated Baire theorem and category equivalence.</p>", "Memory": "<p>Retain Dense-intersection theorem and nowhere-dense definition; prior\nexports E2/v1; keep their assumptions and versions attached.</p>", "Intellect": "<p>Take complements of closures.</p>", "Sense": "<p>The relevant sets are nowhere dense, not merely empty-interior sets\nwithout a closure check.</p>", "Action": "<p>Carry out the numbered deductions and publish E3/v1 together with its\nhypotheses and proof location.</p>"}, "activities": ["<p>If\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A_n</annotation></semantics></math>\nare nowhere dense,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>\\</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">cl</mi><mo>&#8289;</mo></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">G_n=X\\setminus\\operatorname{cl}(A_n)</annotation></semantics></math>\nare open dense.</p>", "<p>Their intersection meets every nonempty open U, so U cannot be\ncovered by\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>∪</mi><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\cup A_n</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>", "<p>Conversely, if some open dense\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">G_n</annotation></semantics></math>\nhave intersection missing a nonempty open U, then\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi>∪</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>\\</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U\\subset \\cup (X\\setminus G_n)</annotation></semantics></math>,\neach complement closed with empty interior, contradicting the category\nproperty.</p>", "<p>Thus the formulations are equivalent.</p>"], "exportId": "E3", "gate": "<p>The relevant sets are nowhere dense, not merely empty-interior sets\nwithout a closure check. Publish only after checking every displayed\ndeduction.</p>", "repair": "<p>If the check fails, revise N3: The relevant sets are nowhere dense,\nnot merely empty-interior sets without a closure check. Withhold this\nterminal result until its gate passes.</p>"}], "edges": [{"source": "N1", "target": "N2", "export": "E1/v1", "id": "T1", "activity": "N2: Completeness supplies a common point", "gate": "<p>Use the same mathematical objects and hypotheses as the source\nexport. Receiving contract: Nested balls and shrinking radii; prior\nexports E1/v1</p>"}, {"source": "N2", "target": "N3", "export": "E2/v1", "id": "T2", "activity": "N3: Category reformulation", "gate": "<p>Use the same mathematical objects and hypotheses as the source\nexport. Receiving contract: Dense-intersection theorem and nowhere-dense\ndefinition; prior exports E2/v1</p>"}], "proof": "<p>Read these steps in order as one proof; the diagram is optional.\nNumbered activities identify the specific constructions and deductions\nowned by each node.</p>\n<h3 id=\"n1-nested-closed-balls-1\">§4.N1 — Nested closed balls</h3>\n<p>Fix a nonempty open set\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U</annotation></semantics></math>\nand a sequence of open dense sets\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">G_n</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li><p>Choose a positive-radius closed ball\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>\ncontained in\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>∩</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U\\cap G_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>,\nwith\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn><mi>/</mi><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r_{1}\\le 1/2</annotation></semantics></math>.</p></li>\n<li><p>This intersection is nonempty open.</p></li>\n<li><p>Inductively the open ball centered at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_n</annotation></semantics></math>\nwith radius\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r_n</annotation></semantics></math>\nmeets\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">G_{n+1}</annotation></semantics></math>;\nchoose a point there and a positive radius\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>≤</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>−</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r_{n+1}\\le 2^{(-n-1)}</annotation></semantics></math>\nso its closed ball\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B_{n+1}</annotation></semantics></math>\nis contained in that intersection.</p></li>\n<li><p>Then\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B_{n+1}\\subset B_n</annotation></semantics></math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>⊂</mo><mi>U</mi><mo>∩</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∩</mo><mi>⋯</mi><mo>∩</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B_n\\subset U\\cap G_{1}\\cap \\cdots \\cap G_n</annotation></semantics></math>.</p></li>\n<li><p>This works also at isolated points, where small balls may be\nsingletons.</p></li>\n</ol>\n<h3 id=\"n2-completeness-supplies-a-common-point-1\">§4.N2 — Completeness\nsupplies a common point</h3>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li><p>For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m&gt;n</annotation></semantics></math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_m</annotation></semantics></math>\nlies in\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B_n</annotation></semantics></math>,\nso\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d(x_m,x_n)\\le r_n\\to 0</annotation></semantics></math>.</p></li>\n<li><p>Thus the centers are Cauchy and converge to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x\\in X</annotation></semantics></math>\nby completeness.</p></li>\n<li><p>For fixedn, all subsequent centers lie in the closed set\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B_n</annotation></semantics></math>,\nso\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x\\in B_n</annotation></semantics></math>.</p></li>\n<li><p>Consequentlyx lies in U and every\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">G_n</annotation></semantics></math>.</p></li>\n<li><p>Since U was any nonempty open set,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>∩</mi><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\cap G_n</annotation></semantics></math>\nis dense.</p></li>\n</ol>\n<h3 id=\"n3-category-reformulation-1\">§4.N3 — Category reformulation</h3>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li><p>If\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A_n</annotation></semantics></math>\nare nowhere dense,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>\\</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">cl</mi><mo>&#8289;</mo></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">G_n=X\\setminus\\operatorname{cl}(A_n)</annotation></semantics></math>\nare open dense.</p></li>\n<li><p>Their intersection meets every nonempty open U, so U cannot be\ncovered by\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>∪</mi><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\cup A_n</annotation></semantics></math>.</p></li>\n<li><p>Conversely, if some open dense\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">G_n</annotation></semantics></math>\nhave intersection missing a nonempty open U, then\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi>∪</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>\\</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U\\subset \\cup (X\\setminus G_n)</annotation></semantics></math>,\neach complement closed with empty interior, contradicting the category\nproperty.</p></li>\n<li><p>Thus the formulations are equivalent.</p></li>\n</ol>", "example": "<p>In R, enumerate rationals\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q_n</annotation></semantics></math>\nand set\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>R</mi><mo>\\</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">{</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">G_n=R\\setminus\\{q_n\\}</annotation></semantics></math>.\nTheir intersection is the irrationals, which the theorem shows are\ndense. Each\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">G_n</annotation></semantics></math>\nis open dense; the argument constructs an irrational inside any chosen\nnonempty interval.</p>", "boundary": ["<p>Q with its usual metric is incomplete and is the countable union of\nsingleton nowhere-dense subsets relative to Q. The complements of its\nenumerated points have empty total intersection. Sense traces the\nfailure to the missing metric limit in X, not to the finite nesting\nsteps.</p>"], "repair": [{"label": "Failure and correction", "html": "<p>This is an authored teaching trace, not a recorded agent run.</p>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li><strong>Ego\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mo>→</mo><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\to</annotation></semantics></math>\nMemory:</strong> register the §1 theorem, imports, and requested exports\nbefore beginning the proof.</li>\n<li><strong>Intellect\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mo>→</mo><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\to</annotation></semantics></math>\nAction:</strong> choose the bridges in §3 and carry out the numbered\nactivities in §4; transmit only established E-exports along the §2\nDAG.</li>\n<li><strong>Sense\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mo>→</mo><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\to</annotation></semantics></math>\nresponsible node:</strong> test the specific boundary/failure in §5 and\nthe node’s individual audit. A failing candidate is withheld; downstream\nnodes may not use it.</li>\n<li><strong>Memory + Intellect:</strong> identify whether the error is\nan omitted condition, an invalid inference, or an attempted extension\nbeyond the theorem. Restore the exact contract or explicitly state a\ndifferent one.</li>\n<li><strong>Action:</strong> redo the affected mathematical activity\nusing the repaired condition or inference. Recheck consuming nodes,\nrather than feeding their conclusions back as premises.</li>\n<li><strong>Ego:</strong> publish only the supported export with its\nassumptions and proof location. A hypothetical revised export is v2; the\nwritten proof here is the v1 exposition, not a claim that this repair\nwas executed.</li>\n</ol>"}, {"label": "Hypothesis boundary", "html": "<p>Q with its usual metric is incomplete and is the countable union of\nsingleton nowhere-dense subsets relative to Q. The complements of its\nenumerated points have empty total intersection. Sense traces the\nfailure to the missing metric limit in X, not to the finite nesting\nsteps.</p>"}, {"label": "Version and recheck", "html": "<p>Treat any changed assumption as a new contract. Recheck the revised\nexport and all of its descendants. A backward diagnostic never\nestablishes an earlier theorem. This is an authored teaching trace, not\nan execution log.</p>"}], "standard": true, "sha256": "8a05ab2803a784e1133b78105e01d8fcf0b46d45e078fb79171ae6e508341e5e", "idea": "<p>A complete authored walkthrough of baire category theorem for\ncomplete metric spaces, with explicit mathematical contracts, local\nverification gates, and reusable exports.</p>", "references": "<ul>\n<li><a\nhref=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/18-901-introduction-to-topology-fall-2004/pages/lecture-notes/\">MIT\nIntroduction to Topology: separation, Urysohn, and Tychonoff\nmaterial</a>.</li>\n</ul>", "pdf": "reports/pdf/baire-category-theorem-for-complete-metric-spaces.pdf", "tex": "reports/latex/baire-category-theorem-for-complete-metric-spaces.tex", "markdown": "markdown/baire-category-theorem-for-complete-metric-spaces.md"}, {"id": "GT-05", "slug": "tychonoffs-theorem-for-compact-hausdorff-spaces", "title": "Tychonoff’s Theorem for Compact Hausdorff Spaces", "target": "<p>Working in ZFC, prove that an arbitrary product\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∏</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\prod_{i\\in I}X_i</annotation></semantics></math>\nof nonempty compact Hausdorff spaces is compact and Hausdorff in the\nproduct topology.</p>", "imports": ["<p>Work in ZFC. Import the ultrafilter extension principle: every proper\nfilter extends to an ultrafilter. Import the compactness criterion: a\nspace is compact iff every ultrafilter on it converges to some point.\nThis follows by applying the finite-intersection property to closed sets\nand, conversely, extending a hypothetical cover-with-no-finite-subcover\ncomplement filter. In a Hausdorff space an ultrafilter has at most one\nlimit. The nonempty product is available by choice.</p>"], "nodes": [{"id": "N1", "title": "Project an ultrafilter", "proof": "1. For eachi defineU_i={A⊂X_i:π_i⁻¹(A)∈U}.\n\n2. Inverse images preserve complements, inclusions, and finite intersections, soU_i is a proper ultrafilter: for eachA exactly one ofA or its complement belongs.\n\n3. Compactness ofX_i gives a limitx_i; Hausdorffness makes it unique.\n\n4. The tuple x=(x_i) is a point ofX.\n\n5. If I is empty the product is a singleton and the result is immediate.", "role": "<p>Push the filter through each coordinate map.</p>", "inputs": "<p><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>∏</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X=\\prod X_i</annotation></semantics></math>\nand an ultrafilterU on X; foundational imports stated in §1</p>", "export": "<p>A coordinate limit tuple for every ultrafilter on the product.</p>", "faculties": {"Ego": "<p>Own E1/v1: A coordinate limit tuple for every ultrafilter on the\nproduct.</p>", "Memory": "<p>Retain\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>∏</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X=\\prod X_i</annotation></semantics></math>\nand an ultrafilterU on X; foundational imports stated in §1; keep their\nassumptions and versions attached.</p>", "Intellect": "<p>Push the filter through each coordinate map.</p>", "Sense": "<p>Convergence is an ultrafilter notion; no sequential compactness\nshortcut is used.</p>", "Action": "<p>Carry out the numbered deductions and publish E1/v1 together with its\nhypotheses and proof location.</p>"}, "activities": ["<p>For eachi define\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">{</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>:</mo><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>−</mi><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>∈</mo><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U_i=\\{A\\subset X_i:\\pi _i^{-1}(A)\\in U\\}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>", "<p>Inverse images preserve complements, inclusions, and finite\nintersections, so\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U_i</annotation></semantics></math>\nis a proper ultrafilter: for each A exactly one of A or its complement\nbelongs.</p>", "<p>Compactness of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X_i</annotation></semantics></math>\ngives a limit\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_i</annotation></semantics></math>;\nHausdorffness makes it unique.</p>", "<p>The tuple\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=(x_i)</annotation></semantics></math>\nis a point of X.</p>", "<p>If I is empty the product is a singleton and the result is\nimmediate.</p>"], "exportId": "E1", "gate": "<p>Convergence is an ultrafilter notion; no sequential compactness\nshortcut is used. Publish only after checking every displayed\ndeduction.</p>", "repair": "<p>If the check fails, revise N1: Convergence is an ultrafilter notion;\nno sequential compactness shortcut is used. Recheck dependent nodes N2,\nN3.</p>"}, {"id": "N2", "title": "Product convergence", "proof": "1. A basic neighborhood ofx has formB=∩_{i∈F}π_i⁻¹(V_i) withF finite andV_i a neighborhood ofx_i.\n\n2. Since U_i→x_i, everyV_i belongs toU_i; hence each inverse image belongs toU.\n\n3. A filter is closed under finite intersections, soB∈U.\n\n4. Any neighborhood contains such aB and is therefore inU.\n\n5. Thus U converges tox.\n\n6. The imported criterion provesX compact.", "role": "<p>Check only finitely many coordinate constraints at a time.</p>", "inputs": "<p>All coordinate limits; prior exports E1/v1</p>", "export": "<p>Every ultrafilter on X converges; X is compact.</p>", "faculties": {"Ego": "<p>Own E2/v1: Every ultrafilter on X converges; X is compact.</p>", "Memory": "<p>Retain All coordinate limits; prior exports E1/v1; keep their\nassumptions and versions attached.</p>", "Intellect": "<p>Check only finitely many coordinate constraints at a time.</p>", "Sense": "<p>A filter need not be closed under infinitely many intersections.</p>", "Action": "<p>Carry out the numbered deductions and publish E2/v1 together with its\nhypotheses and proof location.</p>"}, "activities": ["<p>A basic neighborhood ofx has form\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mo>∩</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow></msub><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>−</mi><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B=\\cap _{i\\in F}\\pi _i^{-1}(V_i)</annotation></semantics></math>\nwith F finite and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V_i</annotation></semantics></math>\na neighborhood of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_i</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>", "<p>Since\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>→</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U_i\\to x_i</annotation></semantics></math>,\nevery\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V_i</annotation></semantics></math>\nbelongs to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U_i</annotation></semantics></math>;\nhence each inverse image belongs to U.</p>", "<p>A filter is closed under finite intersections, so\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B\\in U</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>", "<p>Any neighborhood contains such aB and is therefore in U.</p>", "<p>Thus U converges tox.</p>", "<p>The imported criterion proves X compact.</p>"], "exportId": "E2", "gate": "<p>A filter need not be closed under infinitely many intersections.\nPublish only after checking every displayed deduction.</p>", "repair": "<p>If the check fails, revise N2: A filter need not be closed under\ninfinitely many intersections. Recheck dependent nodes N3.</p>"}, {"id": "N3", "title": "Hausdorff product and synthesis", "proof": "1. Ifx≠y, some coordinatei hasx_i≠y_i.\n\n2. Hausdorffness ofX_i gives disjoint open neighborhoodsV,W.\n\n3. Their projection inverse images are disjoint open neighborhoods ofx,y inX.\n\n4. Hence X is Hausdorff.\n\n5. Together with node2 this proves the arbitrary-product theorem under its stated foundations.", "role": "<p>Separate using one differing coordinate.</p>", "inputs": "<p>Distinct product points; prior exports E2/v1</p>", "export": "<p>The product is compact and Hausdorff.</p>", "faculties": {"Ego": "<p>Own E3/v1: The product is compact and Hausdorff.</p>", "Memory": "<p>Retain Distinct product points; prior exports E2/v1; keep their\nassumptions and versions attached.</p>", "Intellect": "<p>Separate using one differing coordinate.</p>", "Sense": "<p>The topology is the product topology; the box topology has different\nbasic neighborhoods.</p>", "Action": "<p>Carry out the numbered deductions and publish E3/v1 together with its\nhypotheses and proof location.</p>"}, "activities": ["<p>If\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x\\ne y</annotation></semantics></math>,\nsome coordinatei has\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>≠</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_i\\ne y_i</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>", "<p>Hausdorffness of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X_i</annotation></semantics></math>\ngives disjoint open neighborhoodsV,W.</p>", "<p>Their projection inverse images are disjoint open neighborhoods ofx,y\nin X.</p>", "<p>Hence X is Hausdorff.</p>", "<p>Together with node2 this proves the arbitrary-product theorem under\nits stated foundations.</p>"], "exportId": "E3", "gate": "<p>The topology is the product topology; the box topology has different\nbasic neighborhoods. Publish only after checking every displayed\ndeduction.</p>", "repair": "<p>If the check fails, revise N3: The topology is the product topology;\nthe box topology has different basic neighborhoods. Withhold this\nterminal result until its gate passes.</p>"}], "edges": [{"source": "N1", "target": "N2", "export": "E1/v1", "id": "T1", "activity": "N2: Product convergence", "gate": "<p>Use the same mathematical objects and hypotheses as the source\nexport. Receiving contract: All coordinate limits; prior exports\nE1/v1</p>"}, {"source": "N2", "target": "N3", "export": "E2/v1", "id": "T2", "activity": "N3: Hausdorff product and synthesis", "gate": "<p>Use the same mathematical objects and hypotheses as the source\nexport. Receiving contract: Distinct product points; prior exports\nE2/v1</p>"}], "proof": "<p>Read these steps in order as one proof; the diagram is optional.\nNumbered activities identify the specific constructions and deductions\nowned by each node.</p>\n<h3 id=\"n1-project-an-ultrafilter-1\">§4.N1 — Project an ultrafilter</h3>\n<p>Let\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mo>∏</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X=\\prod_i X_i</annotation></semantics></math>\nand choose an arbitrary ultrafilter\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>𝒰</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathcal U</annotation></semantics></math>\non\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li><p>For eachi define\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">{</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>:</mo><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>−</mi><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>∈</mo><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U_i=\\{A\\subset X_i:\\pi _i^{-1}(A)\\in U\\}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p></li>\n<li><p>Inverse images preserve complements, inclusions, and finite\nintersections, so\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U_i</annotation></semantics></math>\nis a proper ultrafilter: for each A exactly one of A or its complement\nbelongs.</p></li>\n<li><p>Compactness of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X_i</annotation></semantics></math>\ngives a limit\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_i</annotation></semantics></math>;\nHausdorffness makes it unique.</p></li>\n<li><p>The tuple\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=(x_i)</annotation></semantics></math>\nis a point of X.</p></li>\n<li><p>If I is empty the product is a singleton and the result is\nimmediate.</p></li>\n</ol>\n<h3 id=\"n2-product-convergence-1\">§4.N2 — Product convergence</h3>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li><p>A basic neighborhood ofx has form\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mo>∩</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow></msub><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>−</mi><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B=\\cap _{i\\in F}\\pi _i^{-1}(V_i)</annotation></semantics></math>\nwith F finite and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V_i</annotation></semantics></math>\na neighborhood of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_i</annotation></semantics></math>.</p></li>\n<li><p>Since\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>→</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U_i\\to x_i</annotation></semantics></math>,\nevery\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V_i</annotation></semantics></math>\nbelongs to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U_i</annotation></semantics></math>;\nhence each inverse image belongs to U.</p></li>\n<li><p>A filter is closed under finite intersections, so\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B\\in U</annotation></semantics></math>.</p></li>\n<li><p>Any neighborhood contains such aB and is therefore in U.</p></li>\n<li><p>Thus U converges tox.</p></li>\n<li><p>The imported criterion proves X compact.</p></li>\n</ol>\n<h3 id=\"n3-hausdorff-product-and-synthesis-1\">§4.N3 — Hausdorff product\nand synthesis</h3>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li><p>If\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x\\ne y</annotation></semantics></math>,\nsome coordinatei has\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>≠</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_i\\ne y_i</annotation></semantics></math>.</p></li>\n<li><p>Hausdorffness of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X_i</annotation></semantics></math>\ngives disjoint open neighborhoodsV,W.</p></li>\n<li><p>Their projection inverse images are disjoint open neighborhoods\nofx,y in X.</p></li>\n<li><p>Hence X is Hausdorff.</p></li>\n<li><p>Together with node2 this proves the arbitrary-product theorem\nunder its stated foundations.</p></li>\n</ol>", "example": "<p>For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo><mi>I</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X=[0,1]^I</annotation></semantics></math>\nwith an arbitrarily large index set, a basic open neighborhood specifies\nfinitely many coordinates. The ultrafilter proof treats any I uniformly;\nit never orders its elements into a sequence. For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I=\\{1,2\\}</annotation></semantics></math>\nit reproduces compactness of the square.</p>", "boundary": ["<p>In the countable box\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mi>r</mi><mi>o</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>u</mi><mi>c</mi><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo><mi>N</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">product[0,1]^N</annotation></semantics></math>,\npoints\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x^{(n)}</annotation></semantics></math>\nwith firstn coordinates0 and all others1 have no convergent subsequence:\nevery coordinate forces any putative subsequence limit to be0, while the\nbox neighborhood\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>∏</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mi>/</mi><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\prod [0,1/2</annotation></semantics></math>)\nexcludes every term. A compact Hausdorff space makes each infinite set\nhave an accumulation point, and this particular set has none: 0 has the\ndisplayed box neighborhood excluding every term; for any other point x,\nchoose a coordinate k with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_k&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>\nand a coordinate neighborhood avoiding 0, which excludes all terms with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n\\ge k</annotation></semantics></math>.\nThe remaining finite terms can be excluded individually by Hausdorff\nneighborhoods, so x is not an accumulation point either. Thus the box\nproduct is not compact. Sense keeps the finite-coordinate product\ntopology rather than substituting infinite intersections.</p>"], "repair": [{"label": "Failure and correction", "html": "<p>This is an authored teaching trace, not a recorded agent run.</p>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li><strong>Ego\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mo>→</mo><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\to</annotation></semantics></math>\nMemory:</strong> register the §1 theorem, imports, and requested exports\nbefore beginning the proof.</li>\n<li><strong>Intellect\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mo>→</mo><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\to</annotation></semantics></math>\nAction:</strong> choose the bridges in §3 and carry out the numbered\nactivities in §4; transmit only established E-exports along the §2\nDAG.</li>\n<li><strong>Sense\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mo>→</mo><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\to</annotation></semantics></math>\nresponsible node:</strong> test the specific boundary/failure in §5 and\nthe node’s individual audit. A failing candidate is withheld; downstream\nnodes may not use it.</li>\n<li><strong>Memory + Intellect:</strong> identify whether the error is\nan omitted condition, an invalid inference, or an attempted extension\nbeyond the theorem. Restore the exact contract or explicitly state a\ndifferent one.</li>\n<li><strong>Action:</strong> redo the affected mathematical activity\nusing the repaired condition or inference. Recheck consuming nodes,\nrather than feeding their conclusions back as premises.</li>\n<li><strong>Ego:</strong> publish only the supported export with its\nassumptions and proof location. A hypothetical revised export is v2; the\nwritten proof here is the v1 exposition, not a claim that this repair\nwas executed.</li>\n</ol>"}, {"label": "Hypothesis boundary", "html": "<p>In the countable box\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mi>r</mi><mi>o</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>u</mi><mi>c</mi><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo><mi>N</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">product[0,1]^N</annotation></semantics></math>,\npoints\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x^{(n)}</annotation></semantics></math>\nwith firstn coordinates0 and all others1 have no convergent subsequence:\nevery coordinate forces any putative subsequence limit to be0, while the\nbox neighborhood\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>∏</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mi>/</mi><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\prod [0,1/2</annotation></semantics></math>)\nexcludes every term. A compact Hausdorff space makes each infinite set\nhave an accumulation point, and this particular set has none: 0 has the\ndisplayed box neighborhood excluding every term; for any other point x,\nchoose a coordinate k with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_k&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>\nand a coordinate neighborhood avoiding 0, which excludes all terms with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n\\ge k</annotation></semantics></math>.\nThe remaining finite terms can be excluded individually by Hausdorff\nneighborhoods, so x is not an accumulation point either. Thus the box\nproduct is not compact. Sense keeps the finite-coordinate product\ntopology rather than substituting infinite intersections.</p>"}, {"label": "Version and recheck", "html": "<p>Treat any changed assumption as a new contract. Recheck the revised\nexport and all of its descendants. A backward diagnostic never\nestablishes an earlier theorem. This is an authored teaching trace, not\nan execution log.</p>"}], "standard": true, "sha256": "51e0feaf7b41df8bcd9ce322ca8f7a006e36b9906490d2cd089e4480cd7699dd", "idea": "<p>A complete authored walkthrough of tychonoff’s theorem for compact\nhausdorff spaces, with explicit mathematical contracts, local\nverification gates, and reusable exports.</p>", "references": "<ul>\n<li><a\nhref=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/18-901-introduction-to-topology-fall-2004/pages/lecture-notes/\">MIT\nIntroduction to Topology: separation, Urysohn, and Tychonoff\nmaterial</a>.</li>\n</ul>", "pdf": "reports/pdf/tychonoffs-theorem-for-compact-hausdorff-spaces.pdf", "tex": "reports/latex/tychonoffs-theorem-for-compact-hausdorff-spaces.tex", "markdown": "markdown/tychonoffs-theorem-for-compact-hausdorff-spaces.md"}], "links": [], "evidence": "Authored proof walkthroughs. No deployed agent execution or formal kernel verification is claimed."};
